WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.480
Death in the "Fast and Furious" movies isn't
entirely permanent.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.310
It's more of a suggestion.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.490
So while it may seem like these characters
have come to their demise by the end of "Fast

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.622
X," how likely are they to appear alive again?

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:18.880
Out of all the characters who meet grisly
fates at the end of "Fast X," John Cena's

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.200
Jakob Toretto seems the most likely to actually
stay dead.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.980
For starters, he's a relatively recent addition
to the franchise, having only debuted in "F9."

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.070
That makes him a bit more expendable, even
though he is a Toretto.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:35.379
Jakob's whole arc in "Fast X" seems to set
him up for the noble sacrifice he makes at

00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:36.130
the end.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:41.219
His uncle-nephew road trip with Little Brian
is the most grounded and emotionally resonant

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:42.670
storyline in the movie.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.500
After seeing Jakob mostly as a vindictive
killer in "F9," he gets to show his lighter,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.574
goofier side.

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:52.649
He does a commendable job keeping Brian's
spirits up during terrifying circumstances,

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:57.160
and he keeps his promise to his brother Dom
by protecting him at all costs.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.430
A sacrifice from Jakob would make his whole
character a bit easier to swallow.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.930
While he does come back around to the side
of good by the end of "F9," he never really

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.528
redeems himself for the many violent and evil
things he's done.

00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:14.520
Killing him off in a heroic way makes it easier
for the series to grapple with that conundrum,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.012
by simply ignoring it.

00:01:16.012 --> 00:01:20.310
So while explosions have rarely been fatal
in the "Fast and Furious" movies, Jakob's

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.345
bomb-car maneuver seems like it may indeed
be the last we see of him.

00:01:25.345 --> 00:01:30.620
Right before the end of "Fast X," the vengeful
Dante Reyes has Agency head Aimes shoot down

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.350
the plane that's carrying Han, Tej, Ramsey,
and Roman.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.299
It's a stunning twist after Aimes initially
appears to ally himself with Dom.

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:42.720
Aimes blasts one of the plane's engines with
a missile launcher, sending it spiraling around

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:43.911
the side of a mountain.

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:48.860
There's a giant explosion, but there's no
shot of the interior as the plane goes down.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.260
So what are the odds that any of these characters
are actually dead?

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.939
Well, at the very least, Sung Kang's Han is
probably safe.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:00.100
The franchise already spent like five movies
killing him, retconning who killed him, and

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.754
then bringing him back from the dead.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:06.973
Taking him out now, offscreen, seems highly
unlikely after all the work put in to resurrect him.

00:02:06.973 --> 00:02:09.022
"So you got my postcard, huh?"

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.680
Plus, "Fast X" ends with Gal Godot's Gisele
Yashar, the love of Han's life, coming back

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.962
from the grave herself.

00:02:15.962 --> 00:02:19.590
There's almost no way the films would kill
him off before he has a chance to reunite

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.237
with her.

00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:25.099
But since he is on the plane, we technically
don't know whether he makes it or not.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:29.099
Also on the plane that Aimes shoots down at
the end of "Fast X" is Roman Pearce, played

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:33.910
by Tyrese Gibson, the de facto funny man of
the Toretto crew since his debut in "2 Fast

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:34.764
2 Furious."

00:02:34.764 --> 00:02:39.030
Roman's "Fast X" arc is all about leadership,
as he struggles to assert himself as more

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.010
than just a comic relief character.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:46.040
He and Tej have a nice little moment of brotherly
love on the plane before they enter the fray,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.150
and that's basically the last we see of either
of them.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.626
So is Roman dead?

00:02:50.626 --> 00:02:51.626
Probably not.

00:02:51.626 --> 00:02:55.379
If Vin Diesel and the writers wanted to kill
somebody on the plane, it probably wouldn't

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:56.662
be the class clown.

00:02:56.662 --> 00:02:57.871
That's just too sad!

00:02:57.871 --> 00:03:01.920
Roman is far from the most essential member
of the gang, but he's been part of the franchise

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.726
since the second movie, and he's way too beloved
to kill off so unceremoniously.

00:03:06.726 --> 00:03:08.816
Plus, he's gotten out of worse scrapes.

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:12.079
"How in the hell are you not dead?"

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:16.650
That said, it wouldn't be outside the realm
of possibility for him to make a big sacrifice

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.890
to save his friends at the start of "Fast
and Furious 11."

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:24.230
Such an act would mesh well with his leadership
arc in "Fast X," and it would kick off the

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.650
next movie with some heavy stakes.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:32.066
Tej, played by Ludarcis, is also on the plane
when Aimes sends it hurtling into a mountain.

00:03:32.066 --> 00:03:33.286
But is he dead?

00:03:33.286 --> 00:03:35.646
Like Roman, the answer is probably no.

00:03:35.646 --> 00:03:39.980
While killing off characters to raise the
stakes is natural at the end of a long franchise,

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.660
killing either half of the classic "Fast and
Furious" comedy duo would be just too tragic.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:49.470
In fact, it seems more likely that Tej would
be the one to save himself and the others

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.743
by figuring out some high-tech way to escape
the explosion.

00:03:52.743 --> 00:03:56.766
Given that Ramsey is there with him too, there's
a good chance they could figure something out.

00:03:56.766 --> 00:03:59.354
For now, though, Tej's fate remains unclear.

00:03:59.354 --> 00:04:03.590
Like Roman, he's been a part of the franchise
since his debut in "2 Fast 2 Furious," and

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.023
it's hard to imagine the movies without him
at this point.

00:04:07.023 --> 00:04:10.000
Last but least on the doomed plane is Ramsey.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.220
From one angle, she could be seen as the most
expendable of the foursome because she's been

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.259
a "Fast and Furious" team member for the least
amount of time.

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:22.150
Ramsey first pops up in "Furious 7" as the
creator of God's Eye, the all-seeing computer

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.750
program that can hack into any camera on Earth.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.840
God's Eye plays a pretty major role in the
plot of "Fast X," and Ramsey even gets to

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.880
show a bit of remorse for how her invention
is being used.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.283
So is she dead?

00:04:33.283 --> 00:04:36.449
Like the others on the plane, maybe, but probably
not.

00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:40.630
Though she is the most recent addition to
the series who's also part of the crash, she's

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:45.690
too established to be relegated to an off-camera
death, and the God's Eye is still a major

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.480
part of the plot going forward.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.289
Plus, the whole franchise has also made a
point in recent years of developing its female

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:54.720
characters more thoroughly and balancing genders
in the cast.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.500
Letty and Gisele came back from the dead,
Mia returned in "F9," and "Fast X" even introduces

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.580
Daniela Melchior's Isabel, sister of the late
Elena Neves.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.680
That doesn't mean that the series still couldn't
kill a woman like Ramsey, but her death doesn't

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.870
seem like the kind that would really play
well.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:16.139
One of the early casualties in "Fast X" is
Luis da Silva's Diogo, a Rio-based street

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:18.950
racer who befriended Dom back in "Fast Five."

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:23.430
When Dom returns to Rio looking for Dante
Reyes, Diogo is there to help him out.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:27.940
Sadly, Diogo appears to die at Dante's hands
during their ensuing street race.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:32.789
The villain attaches bombs to the bottoms
of both Diogo and Isabel's cars, and he detonates

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:33.519
them both.

00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:38.289
Diogo's car goes up in flames first, and he
seems to die instantly, as Dom doesn't stop

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:40.560
to help him out as he does with Isabel.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.143
The question remains, though: If Isabel survived
her explosion, why couldn't Diogo have as well?

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:49.000
There's no real narrative reason to bring
Diogo back in the next movie, as returning

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.950
to Rio in back-to-back entries feels a bit
much.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:56.156
But also, explosions rarely ever kill anyone
in "Fast and Furious."

00:05:56.156 --> 00:05:59.340
Mr. Nobody doesn't show up at all in "Fast
X."

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.410
That's probably because, as of "F9," he's
likely dead.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.030
But his shadow looms large.

00:06:04.543 --> 00:06:06.923
"That's a big entrance for a nobody."

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.819
The former head of the secret international
organization known simply as "The Agency,"

00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:16.932
Mr. Nobody was supposedly killed at the beginning
of "F9" when his plane was attacked by Jakob Toretto.

00:06:16.932 --> 00:06:22.130
In "Fast X," his absence is felt severely
when Aimes takes over the Agency and decides

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.013
to go after Dom and the crew.

00:06:24.013 --> 00:06:28.560
It's later revealed that Aimes has been in
cahoots with Dante Reyes for a decade, though

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.780
the exact nature of their association has
yet to be revealed.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:36.440
"Fast X" also introduces Mr. Nobody's daughter,
Tess, who sides with Dom and fights against

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.380
the Agency from the inside.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.430
You'd think that if Mr. Nobody truly were
still alive, he'd come back to stop all the

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.419
chaos happening at his old job.

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:48.700
However, there's one line in "Fast X" that
suggests he might just be in hiding.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:53.210
While meeting with Dom in Naples, Tess refers
to her father as "missing," with a curious

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.000
pause before saying the word.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.810
Does she know something we don't?

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:59.766
Could Kurt Russell return before the "Fast"
saga is over?

00:06:59.766 --> 00:07:01.833
It seems more than likely.

00:07:01.833 --> 00:07:06.590
While he might have been the big bad of Fast
6, the lesser Shaw brother hasn't played a

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:09.710
big role in the franchise over the last handful
of installments.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:14.280
The injuries he suffers while fighting Dom
and his Family spur his older brother, Deckard,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.742
to seek revenge by killing Han, though that
obviously wasn't quite how it went down.

00:07:18.742 --> 00:07:24.070
Owen pops up again in Fate of the Furious,
this time as an ally to Dom when he and his

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.720
brother rescue little Brian from Cipher's
plane.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.783
Once the Fast &amp; Furious spinoff, Hobbs and
Shaw rolls around, we get this confusing little moment.

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:34.835
"Forced me to kill my own brother!"

00:07:35.063 --> 00:07:39.870
But if her son really is dead, Queenie doesn't
seem to know since she tells Dom in F9,

00:07:40.119 --> 00:07:44.352
"Are you looking for one of me sons?"

00:07:44.352 --> 00:07:46.380
"Not tonight."

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:47.913
So is Owen dead?

00:07:47.913 --> 00:07:48.913
Probably not.

00:07:48.913 --> 00:07:52.520
The most likely explanation is that Deckard
faked his brother's death to culminate in

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.910
a triumphant reveal in a future Fast movie.

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:59.699
Also possible is that the relative lack of
interest in future Hobbs &amp; Shaw movies means

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:04.190
that the spinoff film is non-canonical and
will simply be ignored going forward.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:09.220
Either way, with Deckard speeding off to save
his mother by the end of Fast X, Owen's ambiguous

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.190
fate may be cleared up in the next film.

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:15.836
Elena Neves plays a curious role in the "Fast
and Furious" franchise.

00:08:15.836 --> 00:08:20.659
She's a supporting protagonist in "Fast Five"
and in "Fast and Furious 6," a temporary love

00:08:20.659 --> 00:08:25.590
interest for Dom while Letty is "dead," and
then a point of leverage in "The Fate of the

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.319
Furious" after it's revealed that she had
Dom's baby.

00:08:28.319 --> 00:08:32.539
Elena dies in "Fate" at the command of Cipher,
but she continues to play an important role

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:33.630
in "Fast X."

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:38.690
Her sister Isabel becomes embroiled in Dante's
plot after Dom travels back to Rio, and she'll

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.144
likely return in "Fast and Furious 11."

00:08:41.144 --> 00:08:42.394
But what about Elena?

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:47.060
Is she really dead, or is there some chance
that, like Letty, Han, and now Gisele, she

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:48.740
could be "resurrected"?

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.220
You'd think that she would have done whatever
she could to get back to her son if she were

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:53.220
alive, right?

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:58.310
Well, yes, but there are some clues in "Fast
X" that she might, might, be coming back.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:02.880
In addition to introducing Isabel, who makes
Elena central to the story again, "Fast X"

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.190
starts what seems to be the slow process of
redeeming Cipher.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:10.000
At the very least, it makes her a little less
evil and a little more helpful.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.730
That doesn't necessarily mean that she'll
become part of the family, but her redemption

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:19.407
would definitely be a lot easier to swallow
if her greatest crime turned out to be a lie.

00:09:19.407 --> 00:09:22.089
Okay, this one is a bit of a stretch.

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:25.850
Hernan Reyes gets shot in the head by Luke
Hobbs at the end of "Fast Five."

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:30.560
Not only is it a visible, palpable kill, but
it's the entire setup for Dante's vendetta

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.310
against Dom and the crew.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:35.730
There should be no doubt as to whether or
not Hernan is still alive.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:40.110
But this is "Fast and Furious," where no death
should ever be believed 100%.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:43.500
Let's say, just for the sake of argument,
that Hernan is still alive.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.100
It could actually fit quite nicely with what
Dante does in "Fast X."

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.649
We know that he's a trickster.

00:09:48.649 --> 00:09:52.190
He shows it numerous times throughout the
movie, including at the end when it's revealed

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:54.670
that Aimes has been working for him the whole
time.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.540
What if his revenge quest is actually a ruse
for what's really happening?

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.190
"Do you like surprises?

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.170
I adore them."

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:06.820
In the "Fast Five" flashback sequence that
kicks off the film, Hernan tells Dante that

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.680
he has a plan to make their family richer
and more powerful than ever.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.660
Apparently, Aimes has something to do with
that scheme, though we don't yet know how.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:18.280
Why would Dante wait a full decade to track
Dom and Hobbs down?

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:22.050
Maybe it's because his father is still alive
and they were scheming in the shadows until

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.060
the right moment presented itself.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.300
Hernan Reyes is almost surely dead.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:30.779
But actor Joaquim de Almeida came back for
"Fast X," so what's to stop him from returning

00:10:30.779 --> 00:10:32.190
again in the next film?

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:35.080
"Never accept death when suffering is owed."

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:36.579
Jesse died in 2001.

00:10:36.579 --> 00:10:41.490
He was shot to death by Johnny Tran and Lance
Nguyen all the way back in "The Fast and the

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.230
Furious," right in front of the Toretto house
— or at least, that's what they want you

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.230
to think.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.310
If the core "Fast and Furious" saga truly
is ending, what better way to tie everything

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:52.190
together than bringing Jesse back?

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:55.800
He's the first major character to die in the
series, which makes him more deserving than

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.680
anyone of the now-famous "Fast" resurrection
treatment.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.080
He could even be the one to save Dom and Little
B from the dam, or Roman, Ramsey, and the

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:04.300
others on the plane.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.300
Ridiculous?

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:06.300
Sure.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.940
But in this franchise, it's not outside the
realm of possibility.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.339
Just imagine Jesse, the original computer
genius before Tej or Ramsey ever entered the

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:19.589
picture, working on God's Eye modifications,
or space cars, or hacking into the Agency's mainframe.

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:23.970
Picture him in the climax of the next movie
still driving his beat-up Volkswagen Jetta.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:24.970
Wishful thinking?

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:25.970
Surely.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.700
But again, completely feasible given what
this franchise has done in the past.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.420
It just brought Gisele back — the latest
in a long string of returns that includes

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:34.580
Han and Letty.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.790
Jesse could be the next link in that death-defying
chain.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:41.760
What a joy it would be for fans who've followed
the movies since the first one to see him

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.970
ride back into action after 24 years away.

